+Rollup:
+      --rollup.discovery.v4
+          enables discovery v4 if provided
+
+      --min-suggested-priority-fee <MIN_SUGGESTED_PRIORITY_FEE>
+          Minimum suggested priority fee (tip) in wei, default `1_000_000`
+          
+          [default: 1000000]
+
+      --rollup.txpool-ordering <TXPOOL_ORDERING>
+          Transaction ordering strategy for the mempool.
+          
+          Determines how transactions are prioritized when building blocks. - `coinbase-tip`: Order by tip-per-gas (priority fee, or EIP-8130 coinbase tip / gas limit). Default. - `timestamp`: Order by receive time (FIFO, earlier = higher priority).
+
+          Possible values:
+          - coinbase-tip: Order by unified tip-per-gas (higher bid = higher priority)
+          - timestamp:    Order by receive timestamp (FIFO, earlier = higher priority)
+          
+          [default: coinbase-tip]
+
+      --rollup.txpool-max-inflight-delegated-slots <MAX_INFLIGHT_DELEGATED_SLOTS>
+          Maximum number of inflight EIP-7702 delegated account transactions per sender in the txpool. Reth defaults to 1, which prevents delegated accounts from submitting multiple transactions within a block (e.g. buy + approve in a single Flashblock).
+          
+          We raise the default to 4 (matching the EIP-8130 sender cap). Delegated code can move the account's balance mid-block, so a queued tx can become insolvent before the next canonical update; but that case fails fast — revm rejects on the pre-execution balance check before running any delegated code — so the only cost of a small cap is bounded (linear in the cap) mempool memory and cheap wasted pre-checks per account per block.
+          
+          [default: 4]
+
+      --rollup.mempool-sender-limit <MEMPOOL_SENDER_LIMIT>
+          Maximum inflight EIP-8130 transactions per non-locked sender account
+          
+          [default: 4]
+
+      --rollup.mempool-payer-limit <MEMPOOL_PAYER_LIMIT>
+          Maximum inflight EIP-8130 transactions per count-limited payer account
+          
+          [default: 4]
+
+      --rollup.mempool-trusted-delegation-targets <MEMPOOL_TRUSTED_DELEGATION_TARGETS>
+          Additional operator-trusted delegation targets for balance-bounded locked payers.
+          
+          This is local, non-consensus mempool policy and may intentionally differ between nodes. Only configure implementations whose locked mode prevents ETH outflows other than the gas they sponsor; an unsafe target weakens this node's aggregate payer-balance admission bound.
+
+      --proofs-history
+          If true, initialize external-proofs exex to save and serve trie nodes to provide proofs faster
+          
+          [alias: --proofs]
+
+      --proofs-history.storage-path <PROOFS_HISTORY_STORAGE_PATH>
+          The path to the storage DB for proofs history
+          
+          [alias: --proofs.storage-path]
+
+      --proofs-history.db <PROOFS_HISTORY_DB>
+          The on-disk database backend for proofs history
+
+          Possible values:
+          - rocksdb: Store proofs history in `RocksDB`. Also accepted as `v2`
+          - mdbx:    Store proofs history in `MDBX`
+          
+          [default: mdbx]
+          [alias: --proofs.db]
+
+      --proofs-history.window <PROOFS_HISTORY_WINDOW>
+          The window to span blocks for proofs history. Value is the number of blocks. Default is 15 days of blocks based on 200ms block time. 15 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 5 = `6_480_000`
+          
+          Must be at least 1 block.
+          
+          [default: 6480000]
+          [alias: --proofs.window]
+
+      --proofs-history.prune-interval <PROOFS_HISTORY_PRUNE_INTERVAL>
+          Interval between proof-storage prune runs. Accepts human-friendly durations like "100s", "5m", "1h". Defaults to 15s.
+          
+          - Shorter intervals prune smaller batches more often, so each prune run tends to be faster and the blocking pause for writes is shorter, at the cost of more frequent pauses. - Longer intervals prune larger batches less often, which reduces how often pruning runs, but each run can take longer and block writes for longer.
+          
+          A shorter interval is preferred so that prune runs stay small and don’t stall writes for too long.
+          
+          CLI: `--proofs-history.prune-interval 10m`
+          
+          [default: 15s]
+          [alias: --proofs.prune-interval]
+
+      --proofs-history.verification-interval <PROOFS_HISTORY_VERIFICATION_INTERVAL>
+          Verification interval: perform full block execution every N blocks for data integrity. - 0: Disabled (Default) (always use fast path with pre-computed data from notifications) - 1: Always verify (always execute blocks, slowest) - N: Verify every Nth block (e.g., 100 = every 100 blocks)
+          
+          Periodic verification helps catch data corruption or consensus bugs while maintaining good performance.
+          
+          CLI: `--proofs-history.verification-interval 100`
+          
+          [default: 0]
+          [alias: --proofs.verification-interval]
+
