+Rollup:
+      --rollup.discovery.v4
+          enables discovery v4 if provided
+
+      --min-suggested-priority-fee <MIN_SUGGESTED_PRIORITY_FEE>
+          Minimum suggested priority fee (tip) in wei, default `1_000_000`
+          
+          [default: 1000000]
+
+      --rollup.txpool-ordering <TXPOOL_ORDERING>
+          Transaction ordering strategy for the mempool.
+          
+          Determines how transactions are prioritized when building blocks. - `coinbase-tip`: Order by tip-per-gas (priority fee, or EIP-8130 coinbase tip / gas limit). Default. - `timestamp`: Order by receive time (FIFO, earlier = higher priority).
+
+          Possible values:
+          - coinbase-tip: Order by unified tip-per-gas (higher bid = higher priority)
+          - timestamp:    Order by receive timestamp (FIFO, earlier = higher priority)
+          
+          [default: coinbase-tip]
+
+      --rollup.txpool-max-inflight-delegated-slots <MAX_INFLIGHT_DELEGATED_SLOTS>
+          Maximum number of inflight EIP-7702 delegated account transactions per sender in the txpool. Reth defaults to 1, which prevents delegated accounts from submitting multiple transactions within a block (e.g. buy + approve in a single Flashblock).
+          
+          We raise the default to 4 (matching the EIP-8130 sender cap). Delegated code can move the account's balance mid-block, so a queued tx can become insolvent before the next canonical update; but that case fails fast — revm rejects on the pre-execution balance check before running any delegated code — so the only cost of a small cap is bounded (linear in the cap) mempool memory and cheap wasted pre-checks per account per block.
+          
+          [default: 4]
+
+      --rollup.mempool-sender-limit <MEMPOOL_SENDER_LIMIT>
+          Maximum inflight EIP-8130 transactions per non-locked sender account
+          
+          [default: 4]
+
+      --rollup.mempool-payer-limit <MEMPOOL_PAYER_LIMIT>
+          Maximum inflight EIP-8130 transactions per count-limited payer account
+          
+          [default: 4]
+
+      --rollup.mempool-trusted-delegation-targets <MEMPOOL_TRUSTED_DELEGATION_TARGETS>
+          Additional operator-trusted delegation targets for balance-bounded locked payers.
+          
+          This is local, non-consensus mempool policy and may intentionally differ between nodes. Only configure implementations whose locked mode prevents ETH outflows other than the gas they sponsor; an unsafe target weakens this node's aggregate payer-balance admission bound.
+
+      --proofs-history
+          If true, initialize external-proofs exex to save and serve trie nodes to provide proofs faster
+          
+          [alias: --proofs]
+
+      --proofs-history.storage-path <PROOFS_HISTORY_STORAGE_PATH>
+          The path to the storage DB for proofs history
+          
+          [alias: --proofs.storage-path]
+
+      --proofs-history.db <PROOFS_HISTORY_DB>
+          The on-disk database backend for proofs history
+
+          Possible values:
+          - rocksdb: Store proofs history in `RocksDB`. Also accepted as `v2`
+          - mdbx:    Store proofs history in `MDBX`
+          
+          [default: mdbx]
+          [alias: --proofs.db]
+
+      --proofs-history.window <PROOFS_HISTORY_WINDOW>
+          The window to span blocks for proofs history. Value is the number of blocks. Default is 15 days of blocks based on 200ms block time. 15 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 5 = `6_480_000`
+          
+          Must be at least 1 block.
+          
+          [default: 6480000]
+          [alias: --proofs.window]
+
+      --proofs-history.prune-interval <PROOFS_HISTORY_PRUNE_INTERVAL>
+          Interval between proof-storage prune runs. Accepts human-friendly durations like "100s", "5m", "1h". Defaults to 15s.
+          
+          - Shorter intervals prune smaller batches more often, so each prune run tends to be faster and the blocking pause for writes is shorter, at the cost of more frequent pauses. - Longer intervals prune larger batches less often, which reduces how often pruning runs, but each run can take longer and block writes for longer.
+          
+          A shorter interval is preferred so that prune runs stay small and don’t stall writes for too long.
+          
+          CLI: `--proofs-history.prune-interval 10m`
+          
+          [default: 15s]
+          [alias: --proofs.prune-interval]
+
+      --proofs-history.verification-interval <PROOFS_HISTORY_VERIFICATION_INTERVAL>
+          Verification interval: perform full block execution every N blocks for data integrity. - 0: Disabled (Default) (always use fast path with pre-computed data from notifications) - 1: Always verify (always execute blocks, slowest) - N: Verify every Nth block (e.g., 100 = every 100 blocks)
+          
+          Periodic verification helps catch data corruption or consensus bugs while maintaining good performance.
+          
+          CLI: `--proofs-history.verification-interval 100`
+          
+          [default: 0]
+          [alias: --proofs.verification-interval]
+
+      --upgrade-signal.contract <CONTRACT_ADDRESS>
+          L1 upgrade signal contract or proxy address. Defaults to the network's contract for Base mainnet, Sepolia, and zeronet on base-consensus and unified base nodes
+          
+          [env: BASE_NODE_UPGRADE_SIGNAL_CONTRACT=]
+
+      --upgrade-signal.mode <MODE>
+          Upgrade signal application mode
+
+          Possible values:
+          - metrics-only:  Do not mutate local upgrade schedules; live L1 metrics are still observed
+          - startup-apply: Apply the L1 signal once before startup; live polling remains metrics-only
+          - runtime-admin: Apply the L1 signal before startup, automatically re-apply observed live L1 changes, and expose manual runtime admin refresh
+          
+          [env: BASE_NODE_UPGRADE_SIGNAL_MODE=]
+          [default: metrics-only]
+
+      --upgrade-signal.l1-block-tag <L1_BLOCK_TAG>
+          L1 block tag used to read the upgrade signal contract. Also selects the interval between live contract reads: 15m for finalized, 6m24s for safe, 12s for latest
+
+          Possible values:
+          - finalized: Read at the latest finalized L1 block. Reorg-safe; recommended for production
+          - safe:      Read at the latest safe L1 block
+          - latest:    Read at the latest L1 block. May reorg; useful for devnets without L1 finality
+          
+          [env: BASE_NODE_UPGRADE_SIGNAL_L1_BLOCK_TAG=]
+          [default: finalized]
+
+      --upgrade-signal.l1-rpc <UPGRADE_SIGNAL_L1_RPC>
+          L1 execution RPC URL used to read the upgrade signal contract
+          
+          [env: BASE_NODE_UPGRADE_SIGNAL_L1_RPC=]
+
+      --rpc.forwarding-endpoint <RPC_FORWARDING_ENDPOINT>
+          RPC endpoint used to forward submitted transactions without enabling sequencer mode
+          
+          [env: OP_RETH_SEQUENCER_HTTP=]
+
+      --flashblocks-url <FLASHBLOCKS_URL>
+          A URL pointing to a secure websocket subscription that streams out flashblocks.
+          
+          If given, the flashblocks are received to build pending block. All request with "pending" block tag will use the pending state based on flashblocks.
+
+      --max-pending-blocks-depth <MAX_PENDING_BLOCKS_DEPTH>
+          The max pending blocks depth
+          
+          [default: 3]
+
+      --flashblocks.ping-interval <FLASHBLOCKS_PING_INTERVAL>
+          Interval between flashblocks upstream websocket ping frames
+          
+          [default: 30s]
+
+      --enable-transaction-tracing
+          Enable transaction tracing for mempool-to-block timing analysis
+
+      --enable-transaction-tracing-logs
+          Enable `info` logs for transaction tracing
+
+      --enable-transaction-event-journal
+          Enable durable transaction event journal emission from txpool tracing
+
+      --transaction-event-journal-path <TRANSACTION_EVENT_JOURNAL_PATH>
+          Dedicated JSONL path for durable transaction event journal emission
+
+      --enable-tx-forwarding
+          Enable transaction forwarding for mempool nodes to builder RPC endpoints
+
+      --validity-max-predicates <VALIDITY_MAX_PREDICATES>
+          Maximum validity predicates accepted per validity transaction.
+          
+          Capped at [`DEFAULT_MAX_VALIDITY_PREDICATES`], the fixed wire ceiling the request deserializer enforces. Values above it can never be honored and are rejected at startup rather than silently truncated.
+          
+          [default: 64]
+
+      --validity-signature-mode <VALIDITY_SIGNATURE_MODE>
+          Staged validity signature rollout policy at local forwarding ingress. Query nodes proxy the signature unchanged; the sequencer enforces its own policy
+          
+          [default: off]
+
+      --validity-max-expiry-secs <VALIDITY_MAX_EXPIRY_SECS>
+          Maximum lifetime, in seconds, for a validity transaction
+          
+          [default: 60]
+
+      --builder-rpc-urls <BUILDER_RPC_URLS>
+          Builder RPC endpoints for transaction forwarding (one forwarder per URL), used by mempool nodes
+
+      --tx-forwarding-resend-after-ms <TX_FORWARDING_RESEND_AFTER_MS>
+          Resend transactions that haven't been included after this duration in ms (default: 2 blocks)
+          
+          [default: 4000]
+
+      --tx-forwarding-batch-size <TX_FORWARDING_BATCH_SIZE>
+          Maximum number of transactions per forwarding batch
+          
+          [default: 100]
+
+      --tx-forwarding-max-rps <TX_FORWARDING_MAX_RPS>
+          Maximum RPC requests per second per forwarder (0 = unlimited)
+          
+          [default: 200]
+
+      --tx-forwarding-fifo-percent <TX_FORWARDING_FIFO_PERCENT>
+          Percentage of forwarded transactions picked oldest-first (0-100).
+          
+          The rest are picked by highest tip per gas. 100 is pure FIFO. Only matters while a builder queue is backed up; with no backlog every pending transaction goes in the next batch.
+          
+          [default: 20]
+
+      --tx-forwarding-queue-capacity <TX_FORWARDING_QUEUE_CAPACITY>
+          Per-builder forwarding queue capacity [default: 2x the batch size].
+          
+          A transaction's forwarding order is fixed once it enters this queue, so a deeper queue delays the point where FIFO and priority picks apply.
+
+      --tx-forwarding-batched-insert
+          Send each forwarding batch as one `base_insertValidatedTransactions` call.
+          
+          The builder admits the batch as one pool batch instead of one call per transaction. Every builder in `--builder-rpc-urls` must serve that method before this is enabled.
+
+      --enable-inline-simulation
+          Run `meter_bundle` on the mempool node before inserting into the forwarding pool.
+          
+          Has no effect until the sim-worker path is wired; keep off in production.
+
+      --inline-simulation-workers <INLINE_SIMULATION_WORKERS>
+          Number of in-process `meter_bundle` workers
+          
+          [default: 16]
+
+      --inline-simulation-queue-capacity <INLINE_SIMULATION_QUEUE_CAPACITY>
+          Bounded queue of txs waiting for `meter_bundle`
+          
+          [default: 10000]
+
+      --inline-simulation-timeout-ms <INLINE_SIMULATION_TIMEOUT_MS>
+          Per-transaction `meter_bundle` timeout in milliseconds
+          
+          [default: 2000]
+
+      --enable-metering
+          Enable metering RPC for transaction bundle simulation.
+          
+          Turns on `base_meterBundle`. The native payload builder throttles transactions against resource-unit budgets only when this is set and `--payload.resource-metering-schedule` is a non-empty file. The Flashblocks builder uses `--builder.enable-resource-metering` instead.
+          
+          [env: ENABLE_METERING=]
+
+      --metering.metered-opcodes <METERING_METERED_OPCODES>
+          Comma-separated list of EVM opcodes to track for gas metering (e.g., "SSTORE,SLOAD,KECCAK256"). Precompile gas is always tracked
+
+      --payload.resource-metering-schedule <RESOURCE_METERING_SCHEDULE>
+          JSON file containing the startup resource-metering schedule.
+          
+          The native payload builder throttles transactions against this schedule when `--enable-metering` is set and the file is non-empty. Per-dimension `dryRun` observes a budget without excluding transactions.
+          
+          [env: PAYLOAD_RESOURCE_METERING_SCHEDULE=]
+
+      --payload.rejection-cache-max-capacity <REJECTION_CACHE_MAX_CAPACITY>
+          Maximum number of permanently rejected transaction hashes retained by the native payload builder
+          
+          [default: 100000]
+
+      --payload.rejection-cache-ttl-secs <REJECTION_CACHE_TTL_SECS>
+          TTL in seconds for native payload rejection-cache entries
+          
+          [default: 1800]
+
+      --enable-shadow-indexer
